<SubpageLayout title="Climate Resilience & Coastal Protection — Edorer Ijino" description="How climate adaptation work affects the island and why it matters for visitors — coastal protection, resilience projects, and Nauru's climate priorities through 2035." sectionEmoji="📰" sectionLabel="Edorer Ijino" includeFooter={true} locale="na">
  <p>Climate change is an immediate reality for Nauru. With a maximum elevation of just 65 metres and the majority of infrastructure along the narrow coastal belt, the island is exceptionally vulnerable to rising sea levels and storm surges. Nauru's climate response is set out in two primary documents: its updated Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) covering 2025–2035, and the GEF-backed CARE4N coastal resilience project.</p>

  <h2>What this means</h2>
  <p>Nauru's adaptation effort has two distinct tracks: coastal protection where critical infrastructure sits, and longer-term resilience planning — which for a nation of roughly 21 square kilometres necessarily includes difficult questions about how much coastline can realistically be defended.</p>

  <h2>Key projects and frameworks</h2>
  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>🌊 CARE4N — Coastal Area Resilience Enhancement for Nauru</h4>
    <p>Nauru's most concrete coastal protection effort is CARE4N, supported through the Global Environment Facility (GEF) with the Asian Development Bank as implementing agency. Funding comprises a GEF allocation of USD 2,660,600 together with USD 1,000,000 in co-financing. Nauru's own climate documentation notes that CARE4N will not cover the full cost of all recommended solutions — it is a starting point rather than a complete coastal defence programme.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>📄 Updated Nationally Determined Contribution (2025–2035)</h4>
    <p>Nauru has submitted an updated NDC covering 2025 to 2035 under the Paris Agreement, published in full on the UNFCCC website. The NDC is the authoritative statement of what Nauru itself commits to and requests internationally.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>🏔️ Managed Relocation and the Topside</h4>
    <p>Alongside engineered coastal protection, Nauru's adaptation planning has included discussion of managed relocation of people and assets toward the island's higher interior — the Topside phosphate plateau. This reflects the reality that in some locations, defending the coastline may not be feasible indefinitely.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why this matters for your visit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Coastal works can cause localised access restrictions and minor traffic disruption — ask at your accommodation about current conditions</li>
    <li>Beach access at some points may be affected by works; conditions change, so check locally</li>
    <li>Services and infrastructure on Nauru are limited by island scale — this is a constant, not a temporary condition</li>
    <li>The Topside plateau remains accessible and is one of the island's most striking features</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to verify this information</h2>
  <p>The primary sources are the Global Environment Facility's project page for CARE4N (project ID 11705) and Nauru's Updated Nationally Determined Contribution 2025–2035, published on the UNFCCC website.</p>
  <p><strong>Correction note:</strong> An earlier version of this page described a "2026 Climate Resilience Agreement with the Pacific Island Forum's $500 million Adaptation Fund" providing "an $18 million allocation," including a "3.2-kilometre revetment wall" and a "3 MW solar installation." Those figures could not be verified against any official source and have been removed.</p>

